.TH FOOTY 1 "September 2026" "footy 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
footy \- rank today's football matches by how interesting they are
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B footy
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B footy
lists football fixtures for a given day, ranked by how interesting each match
looks. It reads ESPN's public JSON API, so it needs no API key or account, and
shows no advertising, cookie banners or trackers.
.PP
Each fixture is scored from the league position of both sides, their recent
form, how tight the gap between them is, how many goals the two teams tend to
be involved in, and whether the fixture is a derby. Teams listed in
.I favourites
receive a boost. The score is multiplied by the competition's weight and
rendered as a rating from one to five stars on an absolute scale, so a quiet
day genuinely looks quiet.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-date " " \fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR
Show a specific date instead of today.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tomorrow
Show tomorrow.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-week
Show the next seven days.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-limit " " \fIN\fR
Show only the top \fIN\fR matches.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-min\-stars " " \fIN\fR
Hide matches rated below \fIN\fR stars (1\-5).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-fixtures
Show the next matches for the teams listed in
.IR favourites ,
looking up to three weeks ahead. Only the competitions those teams actually
play in are searched.
.TP
.B \-\-live
Show only matches being played right now, with scores. If nothing is in play,
report the next kickoff instead.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-by\-league
Group by competition and show the best of each, rather than one global
ranking. Useful when following many leagues, as a strong league will
otherwise bury a weaker one.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-table " " [\fILEAGUE\fR]
Show a league table. The name is matched loosely, so
.BR pl ", " laliga ", " champ ", " 2.bl " and " eredivisie
all work. Omit the name for every tracked table.
.TP
.B \-\-notify
Send a desktop notification listing the day's top matches, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch " " [\fIN\fR]
Open the N-th match in the day's ranking in a local IPTV player. The channel
is resolved from a beIN MENA EPG by kickoff time and team names, then matched
to a provider stream at the best available quality; see
.I "WATCHING A MATCH"
below. Omit \fIN\fR for an interactive numbered list.
.TP
.B \-\-playlist
Print an M3U playlist where each match is a channel named after the fixture,
for importing into an IPTV player.
.TP
.B \-\-iptv\-channels
List the IPTV provider's channels, grouped by name and quality, to help fill
in
.IR "iptv.channels"
hints.
.TP
.B \-\-import\-iptvnator
Local one-shot helper: read the Xtream login out of an iptvnator
installation on this machine and write an
.B [iptv]
section into
.I config.toml
(which is gitignored). Never run automatically; the
.B [iptv]
section is always bring-your-own.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print machine-readable JSON.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Force colour even when the output is piped, for example into
.BR less (1)
with \fB\-R\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
A fixture line reads:
.PP
.RS
.EX
****.  17:00  C Palace 18 (H) v Liverpool 13 (A)  PL <3
              LLWLD / DDDLD
.EE
.RE
.PP
The stars are the rating, followed by kickoff in local time (or
.B LIVE
/
.BR FT ),
each club with its league position,
.BR (H) " and " (A)
for home and away, the competition, and
.B <3
if it is a team you follow. The second line is each side's last five results.
.PP
Team names are coloured by who is expected to win: green for the favourite,
amber for the underdog, and purple when it is too close to call. That
expectation comes from league position (70%), recent form (30%) and a small
allowance for playing at home; early in a season the margin required to name
a favourite is widened, because the table has not yet earned that confidence.
Note that this is unrelated to
.IR favourites ,
which marks your own teams with
.BR <3 .
.PP
Colour is truecolor where the terminal advertises it, 256-colour or basic ANSI
otherwise, and is disabled when output is not a terminal.
.SH WATCHING A MATCH
.BI "footy \-\-watch " N
opens the N-th match in a local player. This is an optional, bring-your-own
feature: it only activates when
.I config.toml
has an
.B [iptv]
section with your own IPTV provider credentials, and the core command works
identically without it. The channel is found from a public
beIN MENA EPG (scraped from beinsports.com), which lists the evening's
fixtures with their assigned channels; the programme airing closest to the
match's kickoff that names both teams decides the channel, which is then
matched by name to a stream on the configured Xtream provider and played at
the best available quality. Matches the EPG cannot name fall back to an
interactive channel picker, which remembers the last choice per league in
.IR ~/.cache/footy/watchstate.json .
.PP
Credentials are never fetched automatically. Either paste them into
.I config.toml
under an
.B [iptv]
section (see
.IR config.example.toml ),
or, if iptvnator is installed on this machine, run
.B footy \-\-import\-iptvnator
to copy them across in one shot. They are never printed. The EPG URL is
configurable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config.toml
(in the footy directory) Teams followed, competitions tracked, and their
weights. Edit and re-run;
there is nothing to reload. An
.B [iptv]
section configures the IPTV provider, player, quality preference and EPG
source for
.BR \-\-watch .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/footy
Cached responses. Fixtures are held for five minutes and tables for six hours,
and a stale cache is served if the network is unavailable. The IPTV channel
list and EPG are cached for six hours, and the per-league channel pick is
remembered in
.IR watchstate.json .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FOOTY_CONFIG
Path to an alternative configuration file.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, all colour is suppressed, overriding \fB\-\-color\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
The single best match in each competition tomorrow:
.B footy -t -g -n 1
.TP
Only the standout fixtures this week:
.B footy -w -s 4
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR notify-send (1)
.PP
A daily digest is available as a systemd user timer; see
.I footy.timer
in the project directory.
